[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, okay, I must not do this.

177
00:15:46,857 --> 00:15:47,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, that's greed.

178
00:15:48,057 --> 00:15:48,738
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, what?

179
00:15:49,338 --> 00:15:51,879
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is this greed, or is this what feeling means?

180
00:15:52,619 --> 00:16:00,221
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, you kind of are naturally kind of not on the level of just, you know, embodying it, becoming it.

181
00:16:00,261 --> 00:16:05,443
[SPEAKER_00]: You're developing some perspective in regard to it, without it being this, like,

182
00:16:08,184 --> 00:16:16,206
[SPEAKER_00]: It's easier, in a sense, I think it can be for some people to not become too wound up about not acting out of unwholesome and so on.

183
00:16:18,186 --> 00:16:22,007
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like the two types of thoughts we were talking about last week.

184
00:16:22,047 --> 00:16:23,948
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly, the broad category.

185
00:16:23,988 --> 00:16:31,169
[SPEAKER_03]: But you realize, for that to not be an abstract thing, you need to invest effort, actually, in like clarifying.

186
00:16:31,289 --> 00:16:34,670
[SPEAKER_03]: Undoing things up to the point where you do clearly see

187
00:16:35,462 --> 00:16:39,451
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, anything that partakes in greed or delusion, category of unwholesome.

188
00:16:39,711 --> 00:16:41,816
[SPEAKER_03]: Anything that doesn't partake in it, category of wholesome.

189
00:16:42,177 --> 00:16:43,199
[SPEAKER_03]: Factual, as a phenomenon.

190
00:16:43,239 --> 00:16:45,003
[SPEAKER_03]: You see, you see that nature of it.

191
00:16:46,357 --> 00:16:51,158
[SPEAKER_03]: So, everybody can start that as an idea, and then, out of that external idea, they will try to do it.

192
00:16:51,218 --> 00:16:54,239
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yeah, that's fine, if you, you know, you begin like that.

193
00:16:54,339 --> 00:17:02,581
[SPEAKER_03]: But the point is not to, like, just stick to it blindly, but to actually stop referring to it as an external idea and develop that internal reference point.

194
00:17:02,761 --> 00:17:03,021
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no.

195
00:17:03,041 --> 00:17:08,182
[SPEAKER_03]: I need to know why greed, aversion, delusion are wholesome.

196
00:17:08,322 --> 00:17:14,744
[SPEAKER_03]: How they're always felt, and as we described in the previous talk, they always have a nature of a trap, nature of pressure,

197
00:17:15,753 --> 00:17:22,518
[SPEAKER_03]: Of burden, of debt, and you realize, well, that's factually not good.

198
00:17:23,359 --> 00:17:27,562
[SPEAKER_03]: If it were good, it wouldn't need to be set up in such a devious manner.

199
00:17:28,963 --> 00:17:30,284
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why I don't trust it.

200
00:17:30,304 --> 00:17:31,165
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why I won't do it.

201
00:17:31,846 --> 00:17:33,086
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's something you can see for yourself.

202
00:17:33,106 --> 00:17:34,728
[SPEAKER_03]: That's when the danger becomes apparent.

203
00:17:34,768 --> 00:17:43,595
[SPEAKER_03]: When you suddenly recognize the trap that you haven't been seeing before and you've been walking through and getting trapped many times, now you actually saw it.

204
00:17:44,290 --> 00:17:45,470
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you learn how to see it.

205
00:17:46,671 --> 00:17:49,192
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you avoid it naturally when you see it as a trap.

206
00:17:52,113 --> 00:18:04,477
[SPEAKER_03]: But none of that will just magically crystallize in your mind on account of sticking to the precepts and so on, or meditating diligently in the contemporary sense of technique and concentration.

207
00:18:04,497 --> 00:18:06,618
[SPEAKER_03]: No, that needs... that's the diligence, that's the work.

208
00:18:07,178 --> 00:18:12,780
[SPEAKER_03]: The right type of thinking, abstaining from welcoming the thoughts of unwholesome kind, yeah, picking up dhamma

209
00:18:14,566 --> 00:18:15,347
[SPEAKER_03]: Objects, i.e.

210
00:18:15,868 --> 00:18:20,232
[SPEAKER_03]: dhamma, themes, that you will actually ponder and clarify for yourself.

211
00:18:20,292 --> 00:18:23,555
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, well, the Buddha said, cultivate wholesome, don't cultivate unwholesome.

212
00:18:24,255 --> 00:18:24,976
[SPEAKER_03]: What is wholesome?

213
00:18:26,417 --> 00:18:28,419
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, now, when I have these thoughts, what are they?

214
00:18:28,659 --> 00:18:29,800
[SPEAKER_03]: Are they wholesome or unwholesome?

215
00:18:30,661 --> 00:18:33,444
[SPEAKER_03]: Whatever conclusion you come to, okay, now reinvestigate that.

216
00:18:35,346 --> 00:18:40,070
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you actually have to actively engage in clarifying what work needs to be done.

217
00:18:41,890 --> 00:18:54,539
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, it wouldn't be a trap if if you could kind of, yeah, if you could discern it without that, without that kind of continuous diligence.

218
00:18:54,559 --> 00:18:56,200
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you wouldn't have to go against the grain.

219
00:18:56,260 --> 00:18:56,660
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

220
00:18:56,680 --> 00:18:57,481
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's the point.

221
00:18:57,501 --> 00:19:00,382
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, because, yeah, the trap, the whole point is that it sort of limits you.

222
00:19:00,463 --> 00:19:03,264
[SPEAKER_03]: And people will kind of just accidentally stumble upon enlightenment.

223
00:19:03,465 --> 00:19:06,347
[SPEAKER_03]: I just went in this direction and look, nibbāna.

224
00:19:06,427 --> 00:19:11,490
[SPEAKER_03]: No, it can't happen by accident, because you have to perpetually swim against the whole grain of your being.

225
00:19:11,753 --> 00:19:14,434
[SPEAKER_03]: The entire nature, everything, it points that way.

226
00:19:14,454 --> 00:19:18,256
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, for one reason or the other, you are going against it.

227
00:19:18,416 --> 00:19:19,496
[SPEAKER_03]: You are swimming upwards.

228
00:19:20,516 --> 00:19:21,117
[SPEAKER_03]: For how long?

229
00:19:21,157 --> 00:19:23,878
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, out of faith, for as long as it takes.

230
00:19:24,198 --> 00:19:30,100
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, when things are verified and seen for yourself, then, of course, you won't need any more convincing.

231
00:19:31,981 --> 00:19:33,421
[SPEAKER_01]: This is Angutvig, Nikaya 863.

232
00:19:33,441 --> 00:19:33,822
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh...

233
00:19:39,792 --> 00:19:40,733
[SPEAKER_01]: It's called in brief.

234
00:19:41,494 --> 00:19:56,554
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then a certain bhikkhu approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, and said, Bhante, it would be good if the Blessed One would teach me the dhamma in brief, so that having heard the dhamma from the Blessed One, I might dwell alone, withdrawn, heedful, ardent and resolute.

235
00:19:57,855 --> 00:20:03,699
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha replies, it is in just this way that some hollow men here make requests of me.

236
00:20:04,379 --> 00:20:08,942
[SPEAKER_01]: But when the dhamma has been explained, they think only of following me around.

237
00:20:09,603 --> 00:20:12,184
[SPEAKER_01]: And that, as I was saying, it kind of relates to the last one.

238
00:20:12,604 --> 00:20:17,147
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just reading, you know, suttas after suttas after suttas, but not applying any of it.

239
00:20:17,688 --> 00:20:18,628
[SPEAKER_03]: You just follow the Buddha around.

240
00:20:18,768 --> 00:20:24,872
[SPEAKER_03]: Like you might well be holding a view that reading and getting inspired on account of suttas is the application.

241
00:20:26,053 --> 00:20:26,233
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

242
00:20:26,832 --> 00:20:37,978
[SPEAKER_03]: But practically, you'll just be following the suttas of the Buddha around, not doing it, because needing a constant access to the source of inspiration of the external kind.

243
00:20:38,998 --> 00:20:45,942
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they might not have had the suttas, like all the suttas we have today, but they were still kind of doing the same things.

244
00:20:45,982 --> 00:20:46,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

245
00:20:48,807 --> 00:20:52,648
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the monk says, Bhante, let the best one teach me the dharmain brief.

246
00:20:53,369 --> 00:20:55,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Let the fortunate one teach me the dharmain brief.

247
00:20:55,930 --> 00:20:59,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Perhaps I might come to understand the meaning of the statements.

248
00:21:00,772 --> 00:21:04,253
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, the Buddha says, in that case Bhikkhu, you should train yourself thus.

249
00:21:05,713 --> 00:21:08,875
[SPEAKER_01]: My mind will be immovable and settled internally.

250
00:21:09,695 --> 00:21:12,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Arisen bad, unwholesome states will not overpower my mind.

251
00:21:14,037 --> 00:21:14,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you should train yourself.

252
00:21:16,377 --> 00:21:16,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Do that.

253
00:21:17,258 --> 00:21:17,658
[SPEAKER_03]: There you go.

254
00:21:20,431 --> 00:21:29,814
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you realize why... like, I mean, you can ask yourself why... if that's not enough instruction for you, why is that not enough?

255
00:21:31,115 --> 00:21:34,436
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is that insufficient?

256
00:21:34,456 --> 00:21:43,139
[SPEAKER_03]: You realize, well, it's not because the instruction lacks meat or heartwood, it's because too many obstructions in your mind that this is just too abstract.

257
00:21:44,120 --> 00:21:48,141
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you better start clearing up the mess, so that you can apply that.

258
00:21:49,523 --> 00:21:51,304
[SPEAKER_03]: And everybody starts with some degree of mess.

259
00:21:51,504 --> 00:21:55,968
[SPEAKER_03]: So, everybody has to do some clearing.

260
00:21:56,368 --> 00:21:59,871
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's... and that should be like a... oh, that can be a reflection.

261
00:21:59,891 --> 00:22:04,914
[SPEAKER_03]: Wait, but why is this not enough for my mind to do it, to get inspired?

262
00:22:04,974 --> 00:22:07,116
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I don't truly understand what I need to do.

263
00:22:08,417 --> 00:22:15,962
[SPEAKER_03]: And I don't truly understand, because I cannot measure the task given to me, because I don't quite see where it applies.

264
00:22:16,002 --> 00:22:17,964
[SPEAKER_03]: And I don't see where it applies, because I'm obviously

265
00:22:20,540 --> 00:22:22,321
[SPEAKER_03]: Not listening properly, so to speak.

266
00:22:22,461 --> 00:22:27,443
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, I'm listening to these words, but they don't mean the same thing to me as they meant to the Buddha when he instructed it.

267
00:22:27,503 --> 00:22:33,026
[SPEAKER_03]: When he refers to those things, mind and overpower and unwholesome, but those things are not understood by me.

268
00:22:33,906 --> 00:22:41,890
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I better start understanding that, so that then this instruction can make sense, so that I can measure the task, so that I can actually apply myself and get the results of it.

269
00:22:44,331 --> 00:22:47,873
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's unpleasant work, that's... Yeah, of course.

270
00:22:49,652 --> 00:22:58,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Even just recognizing properly that, wait, I actually don't even know what that means, is kind of a...

271
00:22:59,775 --> 00:23:16,239
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be easy to just kind of take for granted your own, like, first impression of what you think that might mean, and then try and, like, do it, which is obviously gonna result in... That's why the kind of initial that would feel kind of inspiring or something,

272
00:23:16,660 --> 00:23:22,263
[SPEAKER_00]: But then it would sort of run out, and it's like, oh, well, I need to get more instruction, because I don't really...

273
00:23:22,343 --> 00:23:29,587
[SPEAKER_00]: I've done a bit, but now... yeah, now I think I need more, because you didn't clarify in the beginning the...

274
00:23:30,074 --> 00:23:33,855
[SPEAKER_00]: What that initial instruction really meant.

275
00:23:34,315 --> 00:23:39,677
[SPEAKER_03]: And you won't clarify it for as long as you think the clarification comes from the outside.

276
00:23:39,717 --> 00:23:40,117
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

277
00:23:40,617 --> 00:23:43,338
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, sometimes, yeah, maybe you simply don't have enough information.

278
00:23:43,418 --> 00:23:46,079
[SPEAKER_03]: But in cases like this, you have... that's enough, that's sufficient.

279
00:23:46,119 --> 00:23:48,620
[SPEAKER_03]: Otherwise, they wouldn't have given it as a self-sufficient instruction.

280
00:23:49,612 --> 00:24:04,735
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the clarification of that instruction then comes on the level of not further scholastic study, but of actually undoing the basic misconceptions in regard to the phenomenon of mind, consciousness, wholesome, unwholesome,

281
00:24:05,428 --> 00:24:17,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I mean, doing that, you know, trying to figure out what... my mind will be... should be immovable, settled internally.

282
00:24:17,137 --> 00:24:20,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Arisen bad, unwholesome states will not overpower my mind.

283
00:24:20,799 --> 00:24:30,404
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you were actually trying to do that, and doing that, to try and apply that, that instruction will be... you would see deeper meanings to it, and deeper and deeper.

284
00:24:30,444 --> 00:24:34,447
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't move on, because there is depth and the ending of the practice.

285
00:24:34,487 --> 00:24:44,192
[SPEAKER_03]: But another thing to add, though, is that even as a, let's say, simple instruction is that, as in not very complex, not many moving parts,

286
00:24:46,089 --> 00:24:54,291
[SPEAKER_03]: Doesn't matter how much you apply yourself, even if you're doing it what... in the way we were describing it, it won't work unless you have been restrained beforehand.

287
00:24:55,991 --> 00:24:59,512
[SPEAKER_03]: Guarding of the sense doors, keeping the precepts, being watchful and wakeful.

288
00:24:59,872 --> 00:25:01,252
[SPEAKER_03]: It just won't work.

289
00:25:01,312 --> 00:25:03,053
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why the mind will drift away as well.

290
00:25:03,273 --> 00:25:05,133
[SPEAKER_03]: It's because... because you're actually undermining it.

291
00:25:05,213 --> 00:25:13,435
[SPEAKER_03]: You are unanchoring it from the right context to your intentional acts towards sensuality, out of ill-will, out of hindrances.

292
00:25:15,195 --> 00:25:24,660
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you actually invest the work there, you won't need much of any external inspiration to keep you inspired on a topic or a theme of practice.

293
00:25:24,800 --> 00:25:29,843
[SPEAKER_03]: The mind won't drift too far away from it, because you are not taking it too far away from it with your actions.

294
00:25:35,308 --> 00:25:45,975
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's another thing to consider if, like, instruction like this, but just seems to kind of pass over my head and mind doesn't leap, leap into it and wants to think about it, wants to contemplate it.

295
00:25:45,995 --> 00:25:51,058
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I think you might have to re-review your behavior by body, by speech, by mind.

296
00:25:51,118 --> 00:25:52,739
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you spend the rest of your waking day?

297
00:25:52,759 --> 00:25:56,522
[SPEAKER_03]: What do you... things you think about, things you're saying, bodily actions.

298
00:25:57,322 --> 00:25:59,584
[SPEAKER_03]: Are they restrained thoroughly, withdrawn, dried up?

299
00:26:00,924 --> 00:26:02,425
[SPEAKER_03]: If not, well, you got your answer.

300
00:26:03,386 --> 00:26:04,567
[SPEAKER_03]: That's when all of this will apply.

301
00:26:07,180 --> 00:26:16,046
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, the sutta continues, and it's... this... the Buddha gives extra further meditation exercises, some instructions.

302
00:26:17,167 --> 00:26:28,695
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, when a bhikkhu... when bhikkhu, your mind is immovable and settled internally, and arisen bad unwholesome states do not overpower your mind, then you should train yourself thus further.

303
00:26:29,355 --> 00:26:29,615
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

304
00:26:31,497 --> 00:26:32,457
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you've got to do that first.

305
00:26:32,477 --> 00:26:34,379
[SPEAKER_01]: So, have you passed that?

306
00:26:34,399 --> 00:26:35,459
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do you do there first?

307
00:26:35,820 --> 00:26:36,400
[SPEAKER_03]: You do the...

308
00:26:37,692 --> 00:26:38,652
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you measure?

309
00:26:39,032 --> 00:26:41,313
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you know you're not overpowered by the...

310
00:26:41,553 --> 00:26:43,434
[SPEAKER_03]: So, arisen unwholesome states.

311
00:26:43,494 --> 00:26:45,834
[SPEAKER_03]: Your mind is settled, but wholesome states arose.

312
00:26:46,834 --> 00:26:49,415
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's not up to you, as we say often.

313
00:26:50,756 --> 00:26:53,756
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, so is this overpowering me, or is it not?

314
00:26:53,776 --> 00:26:54,176
[SPEAKER_03]: What do I do?

315
00:26:54,216 --> 00:26:55,537
[SPEAKER_03]: How do I know?

316
00:26:55,677 --> 00:26:56,477
[SPEAKER_03]: Am I still invulnerable?

317
00:26:56,517 --> 00:26:57,097
[SPEAKER_03]: Is this moving me?

318
00:26:57,857 --> 00:27:00,358
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how do you know whether you're overpowered or not?

319
00:27:00,718 --> 00:27:02,239
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the measure of being overpowered?

320
00:27:03,119 --> 00:27:06,980
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you just blink, and the next moment is, oh, I'm overpowered.

321
00:27:09,530 --> 00:27:11,051
[SPEAKER_02]: Acting out by body, speech or mind.

322
00:27:11,171 --> 00:27:11,591
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

323
00:27:12,411 --> 00:27:14,232
[SPEAKER_03]: Certainly, by body and speech.

324
00:27:14,752 --> 00:27:16,133
[SPEAKER_03]: You're overpowered if you act out.

325
00:27:16,253 --> 00:27:17,774
[SPEAKER_03]: If you don't act out, you're not overpowered.

326
00:27:17,794 --> 00:27:21,735
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't matter the amount of doubt that might try to make you act out or clarify it.

327
00:27:21,775 --> 00:27:24,237
[SPEAKER_03]: You realize, well, I'm not acting out.

328
00:27:24,797 --> 00:27:26,458
[SPEAKER_03]: Thus, I'm not the one being moved.

329
00:27:26,998 --> 00:27:30,799
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure, I'm feeling the pressure of the unwholesome state, but I'm not acting out.

330
00:27:30,859 --> 00:27:32,140
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm not doing anything on account of it.

331
00:27:32,380 --> 00:27:32,800
[SPEAKER_03]: It's there.

332
00:27:32,960 --> 00:27:33,701
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm mindful of it.

333
00:27:33,881 --> 00:27:34,241
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

334
00:27:36,233 --> 00:27:38,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Internally calm, in regards... Yeah.

335
00:27:38,735 --> 00:27:41,037
[SPEAKER_03]: Internally calm means, basically, not acting out.

336
00:27:41,337 --> 00:27:45,741
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't mean, internally, not feeling the pressure of arisen unwholesome states.

337
00:27:45,801 --> 00:27:49,924
[SPEAKER_03]: Otherwise, why would you need to practice steadiness and not being overwhelmed by it?

338
00:27:50,144 --> 00:27:51,505
[SPEAKER_03]: No, you do feel the weight of it.

339
00:27:51,545 --> 00:27:52,366
[SPEAKER_03]: That's kind of the point.

340
00:27:53,086 --> 00:27:55,328
[SPEAKER_03]: So, so it's like not, not acting out.

341
00:27:55,948 --> 00:27:56,889
[SPEAKER_03]: Not acting out of the body.

342
00:27:57,370 --> 00:27:58,370
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I acted out by speech.

343
00:27:58,550 --> 00:27:58,851
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

344
00:27:58,871 --> 00:28:01,473
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, to that extent, I was moved, but at least I didn't act by the body.

345
00:28:02,515 --> 00:28:03,436
[SPEAKER_03]: I didn't act out by the body.

346
00:28:03,516 --> 00:28:04,617
[SPEAKER_03]: I didn't act out by the speech.

347
00:28:05,138 --> 00:28:08,461
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, mentally, I'm still trying to revolve around and get rid of this doubt.

348
00:28:08,641 --> 00:28:09,822
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm acting out to that extent.

349
00:28:10,002 --> 00:28:11,263
[SPEAKER_03]: Let me stop that now as well.

350
00:28:11,544 --> 00:28:13,466
[SPEAKER_03]: At least I didn't act out by speech and by body.

351
00:28:14,967 --> 00:28:20,032
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it is a gradual training that then you can perfect to the point there is a pressure of the unwholesome.

352
00:28:20,212 --> 00:28:21,133
[SPEAKER_03]: You're internally calm.

353
00:28:21,593 --> 00:28:26,298
[SPEAKER_03]: There is the doubt to clarify whether you're internally calm or not and you recognize even that now.

354
00:28:26,902 --> 00:28:34,626
[SPEAKER_03]: If you were to clarify, you would be acting out on account of discomfort, and you realize, well, by not clarifying it, I'm clarifying it.

355
00:28:35,267 --> 00:28:39,829
[SPEAKER_03]: By not engaging with a clarification of my doubt, I'm staying unmoved.

356
00:28:40,209 --> 00:28:43,091
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's kind of the point that even doubt wants me to resolve.

357
00:28:45,192 --> 00:28:48,214
[SPEAKER_03]: So you stop using the middleman of hindrances and acting out of them.

358
00:28:49,875 --> 00:28:50,115
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

359
00:28:51,704 --> 00:28:56,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Once that's done, once you... Yeah.

360
00:28:56,267 --> 00:28:57,829
[SPEAKER_03]: Unmoved by these things.

361
00:28:59,008 --> 00:29:01,731
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, then you can do these further exercises.

362
00:29:02,091 --> 00:29:05,274
[SPEAKER_01]: And one of them is that Brahmaviharas.

363
00:29:05,474 --> 00:29:16,884
[SPEAKER_01]: I will develop and cultivate the liberation of mind by friendliness or non-ill will, make it a vehicle, a habit, a basis, carry it out, consolidate it and properly undertake it.

364
00:29:17,424 --> 00:29:24,050
[SPEAKER_03]: I would take up a theme that now my mind would listen and pay attention to and adhere to.

365
00:29:24,411 --> 00:29:24,671
[SPEAKER_03]: Why?

366
00:29:24,711 --> 00:29:25,612
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, because I attained it.

367
00:29:27,358 --> 00:29:37,864
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can see, we always say, like, you know, the jhāna, the whole practice comes as a result of you overcoming the hindrances, understanding the unwholesome, thoroughly withdrawing it.

368
00:29:37,904 --> 00:29:44,288
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, every description of jhāna is thoroughly withdrawn from unwholesome states, from five hindrances.

369
00:29:44,929 --> 00:29:50,752
[SPEAKER_03]: On a later occasion, basically, then, he dwells, he enters, he dwells having entered jhāna.

370
00:29:51,993 --> 00:29:55,015
[SPEAKER_03]: On a later occasion, after he's thoroughly withdrawn,

371
00:29:55,405 --> 00:29:56,166
[SPEAKER_03]: Quite secluded.

372
00:29:56,206 --> 00:30:06,173
[SPEAKER_03]: Whichever translation you want to use, it means you've done this work first, thoroughly, without any residue, without any tolerating of the hindrances, and acting out of them.

373
00:30:06,693 --> 00:30:10,416
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, the mind will listen, and it will be inwardly calm,

374
00:30:10,880 --> 00:30:14,682
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, whether it's jhāna or brahma-vihāra, that's the order of development.

375
00:30:15,202 --> 00:30:23,987
[SPEAKER_03]: But not like, oh, I'll practice for jhāna, jhānic ecstatic absorption pleasure, and thus sensuality will fall away as a result of that.

376
00:30:24,388 --> 00:30:25,208
[SPEAKER_03]: And this is the same.

377
00:30:25,248 --> 00:30:30,251
[SPEAKER_03]: People's like, oh, I'm full of will-will, so I'll practice metta, so that these things will fall away.

378
00:30:30,311 --> 00:30:30,751
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no.

379
00:30:31,151 --> 00:30:36,214
[SPEAKER_03]: You gotta drop those things first, and then the mind can develop metta and so on.

380
00:30:37,555 --> 00:30:37,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

381
00:30:37,955 --> 00:30:39,496
[SPEAKER_01]: You, you, you create that.

382
00:30:41,288 --> 00:30:42,549
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, of course, of course.

383
00:30:42,569 --> 00:30:43,109
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that.

384
00:30:43,629 --> 00:30:52,735
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't just magically... No, as we said in the previous one, like, I have abandoned, I have relinquished, I've given up the five hindrances, that nandamata sutta.

385
00:30:54,076 --> 00:30:57,898
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, she... She's given them up, through fully understanding them.

386
00:30:58,158 --> 00:31:00,220
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why now, she has jhānas at will.

387
00:31:01,230 --> 00:31:02,191
[SPEAKER_03]: Not other way around.

388
00:31:03,072 --> 00:31:05,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and likewise with this one.

389
00:31:05,154 --> 00:31:10,218
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't say he develops brahma viharas and then his mind will be immovable.

390
00:31:11,179 --> 00:31:12,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

391
00:31:12,300 --> 00:31:14,502
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, here is a technique for brahma vihara.

392
00:31:15,003 --> 00:31:19,247
[SPEAKER_03]: Stick to it blindly and you'll become immovable like a rock and no hindrance can enter you and

393
00:31:20,087 --> 00:31:25,790
[SPEAKER_03]: And no work of contemplation and enduring the pain or the pressure of unreasoned states that are trying to overwhelm you, you will have to endure.

394
00:31:26,451 --> 00:31:27,491
[SPEAKER_03]: Wouldn't that be wonderful?

395
00:31:28,072 --> 00:31:30,713
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but that's not the case, you know, until you snap a bit.

396
00:31:31,133 --> 00:31:33,595
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, because basically you're just carving things up.

397
00:31:36,877 --> 00:31:56,722
[SPEAKER_01]: And, it goes on further, it says, when this samādhi has been developed and cultivated by you in this way, that's a composure of Brahmavihārus, mind of non-ill-will and so on, then you should develop this kind of samādhi with thinking and pondering.

398
00:31:57,563 --> 00:32:00,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Without thinking, but only just pondering.

399
00:32:00,826 --> 00:32:02,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Develop it without thinking and pondering.

400
00:32:02,687 --> 00:32:09,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Develop it with joy, or letting go of joy, accompanied with ease, or accompanied with equanimity.

401
00:32:09,433 --> 00:32:11,854
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he's doing jhānas on top.

402
00:32:12,115 --> 00:32:16,718
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, he's able to practice jhānas alongside Brahmaviharas.

403
00:32:20,982 --> 00:32:26,006
[SPEAKER_01]: And when bhikkhu, this kind of, so now, when you're able to do that, even if it's steadier,

404
00:32:27,537 --> 00:32:28,958
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then you're qualified for satipaṭṭhāna.

405
00:32:28,998 --> 00:32:29,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

406
00:32:29,238 --> 00:32:32,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindful having removed longing and concern in regard to the world.

407
00:32:52,748 --> 00:33:08,315
[SPEAKER_03]: And while you're doing that, also do... Because by that time, there will be absolutely no obstruction or misconception left, as we started this talk with, in regard to what the body is, what the phenomenon of the body is, what the phenomenon of the feeling is, intentions, mind.

408
00:33:09,135 --> 00:33:16,078
[SPEAKER_03]: It would have all been cleared and clarified and endured sufficiently for you to become very familiar with its nature.

409
00:33:26,553 --> 00:33:27,093
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

410
00:33:27,854 --> 00:33:29,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Make it your vehicle.

411
00:33:29,675 --> 00:33:32,616
[SPEAKER_03]: You travel around with that theme.

412
00:33:32,836 --> 00:33:40,720
[SPEAKER_03]: But the thing is, again, it's not just a theme on the level of, oh, yeah, I can understand what the description of what is body, for what it is, its nature.

413
00:33:40,760 --> 00:33:40,960
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no.

414
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:42,101
[SPEAKER_03]: It's seeing that.

415
00:33:42,641 --> 00:33:44,302
[SPEAKER_03]: It started as an external instruction.

416
00:33:45,282 --> 00:33:48,344
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, the unknown ability, what body is subjected to.

417
00:33:48,364 --> 00:33:50,225
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like positioning this world.

418
00:33:50,645 --> 00:33:52,966
[SPEAKER_03]: Seeing that, when you look at this body, that's what you see.

419
00:33:55,196 --> 00:34:01,479
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's a direct recognition of what you learned from before through the mind development described that precedes it all.

420
00:34:04,260 --> 00:34:09,182
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you're not just applying these abstract notions of impermanence and anicca and so on.

421
00:34:09,202 --> 00:34:14,944
[SPEAKER_03]: They will somehow permeate these things and dissolve the roots of greed, aversion and delusion.

422
00:34:15,384 --> 00:34:16,285
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no.

423
00:34:16,305 --> 00:34:17,145
[SPEAKER_03]: You need to pull them out.

424
00:34:17,165 --> 00:34:17,905
[SPEAKER_03]: You need to find them.

425
00:34:18,906 --> 00:34:19,526
[SPEAKER_03]: Cut for them.

426
00:34:19,646 --> 00:34:20,106
[SPEAKER_03]: Find them.

427
00:34:21,227 --> 00:34:22,027
[SPEAKER_03]: Take them all out.

428
00:34:24,527 --> 00:34:25,607
[SPEAKER_03]: One by one, basically.

429
00:34:27,668 --> 00:34:42,933
[SPEAKER_03]: Until all the work's done, and then we can rest.

430
00:34:42,953 --> 00:34:50,156
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sutta ends by saying, when bhikkhu, this composure, this samādhi has been developed,

431
00:34:51,350 --> 00:34:52,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Well developed by you.

432
00:34:52,793 --> 00:35:00,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, whenever you walk, you will walk at ease, wherever you stand, sit or lie down, you will lie down at ease.

433
00:35:04,130 --> 00:35:04,831
[SPEAKER_01]: No more problems.

434
00:35:05,052 --> 00:35:05,653
[SPEAKER_01]: No more problems.

